# Infrastructure

Everything is created in one resource group,
`rg-${{ values.projectName }}-${{ 'prod' if values.environment == 'production' else values.environment }}-${{ values.azureRegion or 'eastus' }}`.
Names of resources that must be unique across Azure end in a random
six-character suffix that Terraform generates on the first apply.

## Request path

{% if values.enableWaf %}Client → Front Door Premium (WAF) → API Management (Consumption) → function app{% else %}Client → API Management (Consumption) → function app{% endif %}

The function app can also be reached on its own hostname. The gateway
authenticates to it with the function app's host key, so the handlers must
use the authorisation level `function`: with `anonymous` a caller can go
around the gateway.

## What is provisioned

### Function

| Form option | Result |
| ----------- | ------ |
{%- if values.runtime == 'python313' %}
| Runtime `python313` | Python 3.13 |
{%- elif values.runtime == 'dotnet10' %}
| Runtime `dotnet10` | .NET 10, isolated worker model |
{%- elif values.runtime == 'java21' %}
| Runtime `java21` | Java 21 |
{%- elif values.runtime == 'go126' %}
| Runtime `go126` | Custom handler. Azure Functions has no Go worker: the package must contain the compiled Linux binary and a `host.json` whose `customHandler.description.defaultExecutablePath` names it. The Go version is the one the binary was built with. |
{%- else %}
| Runtime `nodejs24` | Node.js 24 |
{%- endif %}
{%- if slots %}
| Deployment strategy `${{ values.deploymentStrategy }}` | Linux function app on an Elastic Premium plan (`premium_plan_sku`, default `EP1`) with a deployment slot `staging`. See [Releasing a new version](#releasing-a-new-version). |
| Memory ${{ memory }} MB | Not applied. Deployment slots need the Elastic Premium plan, whose smallest instance (`EP1`) has 3.5 GB. `premium_plan_sku` selects a larger instance. |
{%- else %}
| Deployment strategy `all-at-once` | Function app on a Flex Consumption plan (`FC1`). A deployment replaces the running version. |
| Memory ${{ memory }} MB | `function_memory_mb = ${{ 512 if memory <= 512 else 2048 }}`. Flex Consumption instances come with 512, 2048 or 4096 MB{% if not (memory in [512, 2048]) %}; ${{ memory }} MB is rounded up to the next size{% endif %}. |
{%- endif %}
| Timeout ${{ timeout }} s | `function_timeout_seconds = ${{ timeout }}`: the host setting `functionTimeout` (app setting `AzureFunctionsJobHost__functionTimeout`) and the time the gateway waits for the function{% if timeout > 230 %}. Azure closes an HTTP request to a function after 230 seconds, so the gateway waits 230 seconds; the full ${{ timeout }} seconds apply to work that is not bound to an HTTP response{% endif %}. |

The function app has a system-assigned managed identity{% if slots %} per slot{% endif %} and a storage
account for the Functions host (`st…fn…`).

### API

| Form option | Result |
| ----------- | ------ |
{%- if graphql %}
| API type `graphql` | Pass-through GraphQL API in API Management. The schema is `graphql/schema.graphql`; requests are validated against it (at most 100 KB and 10 levels deep) and forwarded to the function route `/api/graphql`. Replace the placeholder schema with the one the function implements. |
{%- else %}
| API type `${{ values.apiType }}` | HTTP API in API Management with one wildcard operation (`/*`) for each of GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE, forwarded to the function under `/api`. API Management has one kind of HTTP API: `rest` and `http` give the same result on Azure. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCors %}
| CORS enabled | `cors` policy that answers preflight requests. Origins come from `cors_allowed_origins` (default `*`); all methods and headers are allowed, credentials are not. |
{%- else %}
| CORS disabled | No `cors` policy: browsers on other origins cannot call the API. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableThrottling %}
| Throttling, ${{ values.throttleRateLimit or 1000 }} requests/s | `rate-limit` policy: `throttle_rate_limit` calls per second, then HTTP 429. {% if values.authType == 'api-key' %}The limit is counted per subscription key.{% else %}The limit is counted for all callers together; the Consumption tier has no per-client limit without subscription keys.{% endif %} |
| Burst limit ${{ values.throttleBurstLimit or 2000 }} | `limit-concurrency` policy: at most `throttle_burst_limit` requests are in flight to the function at the same time, further ones get HTTP 429. API Management has no token bucket, so the burst limit caps concurrency instead. |
{%- else %}
| Throttling disabled | No `rate-limit` or `limit-concurrency` policy. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.authType == 'none' %}
| Authentication `none` | The API is public: no subscription key and no token validation. |
{%- elif values.authType == 'api-key' %}
| Authentication `api-key` | The API requires a subscription key in the `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key` header. One subscription is created; its key is the output `api_subscription_key`. |
{%- elif values.authType == 'jwt' %}
| Authentication `jwt` | `validate-jwt` policy against the Microsoft Entra ID tenant (`entra_tenant_id`, default: the tenant of the deploying identity). A token must carry the audience `api_audience` (default `api://${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment }}`). The app registration that exposes this audience is not created here; set `api_audience` to the Application ID URI or client ID of yours. |
{%- elif values.authType == 'oauth2' %}
| Authentication `oauth2` | `validate-jwt` policy against an external identity provider: signing keys and issuer come from `oauth2_openid_configuration_url`, the audience from `oauth2_audience`. Both variables are required. |
{%- elif values.authType == 'iam' %}
| Authentication `iam` | `validate-azure-ad-token` policy: callers are applications and managed identities of the Entra ID tenant (`entra_tenant_id`) that present a token for `api_audience` (default `api://${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment }}`). `allowed_client_application_ids` restricts the callers to the listed client IDs. The app registration that exposes the audience is not created here. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableWaf %}
| WAF enabled | Front Door Premium profile with a WAF policy in prevention mode (Microsoft default rule set 2.1 and bot manager rule set 1.1) in front of API Management. The API policy rejects requests without this profile's `X-Azure-FDID` header, so the gateway URL cannot be used to go around the WAF. The public URL is the Front Door endpoint (output `api_url`). |
{%- else %}
| WAF disabled | No Front Door; the public URL is the API Management gateway URL. |
{%- endif %}

All of these policies are in `policies/api.xml.tftpl`, which Terraform fills
in and attaches to the API.

### Data
{% if not (values.enableDatabase or values.enableCache or values.enableStorage) %}
No database, cache or object storage was selected.
{%- else %}
| Form option | Result | Function app settings |
| ----------- | ------ | --------------------- |
{%- if cosmos %}
| Database `nosql` | Cosmos DB account in serverless mode with a database `app` and a container `items` (partition key `/id`). Account keys are disabled; the function's identity has the role *Cosmos DB Built-in Data Contributor*. | `COSMOS_ENDPOINT`, `COSMOS_DATABASE`, `COSMOS_CONTAINER` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if sql %}
| Database `sql` | Azure SQL server with a database `app` on the General Purpose serverless tier (`GP_S_Gen5_1`, 0.5 to 1 vCore, paused after 60 idle minutes). The administrator password is generated and stored in a Key Vault (secret `sql-administrator-password`). The Entra ID administrator is `sql_entra_admin_object_id` (default: the deploying identity). | `SQL_SERVER`, `SQL_DATABASE` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCache %}
| Cache enabled | Azure Managed Redis (`redis_sku`, default `Balanced_B0`) with a single endpoint (clustering policy `EnterpriseCluster`), TLS only, access keys disabled; the function's identity is assigned the default access policy and signs in with Entra ID. Azure Cache for Redis is not used: it retires on 30 September 2028 and is closed to tenants that had no instance before 1 April 2026. High availability is on in `production` only. | `REDIS_HOST`, `REDIS_PORT` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableStorage %}
| Object storage enabled | Storage account (`st…da…`) with a private blob container `data`. Account keys are disabled; the function's identity has the role *Storage Blob Data Contributor* on the container. | `STORAGE_BLOB_ENDPOINT`, `STORAGE_CONTAINER_NAME` |
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if sql %}

Azure SQL has no role assignment for database access. To let the function
connect with its managed identity, an Entra ID administrator of the server
runs this once in the database `app` (the user name is the function app's
name, output `function_app_name`):

```sql
CREATE USER [<function app name>] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;
ALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [<function app name>];
ALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [<function app name>];
```
{%- if slots %}

The staging slot has its own identity, named `<function app name>/slots/staging`;
create a user for it in the same way.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if cosmos or sql or values.enableStorage %}

Resources that hold application data have `prevent_destroy = true`. Remove
that line from `data.tf` before destroying the environment.
{%- endif %}

### Observability

| Form option | Result |
| ----------- | ------ |
| Log retention ${{ retention }} days | Log Analytics workspace with `log_retention_days = ${{ retention if retention >= 30 else 30 }}`; it receives the function app's logs (`FunctionAppLogs`){% if insights %} and the Application Insights data{% endif %}{% if retention < 30 %}. Log Analytics keeps data for at least 30 days, so ${{ retention }} days is raised to 30{% endif %}. |
{%- if values.enableTracing %}
| Tracing enabled | Application Insights connected to the function app and to API Management: the gateway records every request and passes the W3C trace context to the function, so a request shows as one trace. |
{%- elif values.enableMetrics %}
| Tracing disabled | Application Insights exists because custom metrics are stored there. The gateway records no requests in it, and dependency tracking and trace correlation are switched off in the function host. |
{%- else %}
| Tracing disabled | No Application Insights. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableMetrics %}
| Custom metrics enabled | The gateway emits the custom metric `Requests` (namespace `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment }}`, dimensions API ID and Operation ID) to Application Insights. The function app has the Application Insights connection string, so metrics tracked in the function's code arrive in the same place. To chart custom metrics by dimension, switch on *Custom metrics with dimensions* in the Application Insights resource (Usage and estimated costs); Terraform has no setting for it. |
{%- else %}
| Custom metrics disabled | No `emit-metric` policy; platform metrics only. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableAlarms %}
| Alarms enabled | Action group and three metric alerts: 5xx responses of the gateway, average gateway duration above 80% of the gateway timeout, 5xx responses of the function app. Notifications go to `alert_email`; while it is empty the alerts fire without notifying anyone. |
{%- else %}
| Alarms disabled | No alerts and no action group. |
{%- endif %}

