## Function package

This repository creates the function app without code; the first apply needs
no package. The code is built in the application's repository and deployed
as a zip package.
{%- if values.runtime == 'go126' %}
For Go the package contains the compiled binary (`GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64`),
`host.json` with the `customHandler` section and one folder with a
`function.json` per function.
{%- endif %}
{%- if graphql %}
The function has to serve GraphQL on the HTTP route `graphql` (`/api/graphql`).
{%- endif %}
{%- if not slots %}

Either of these supplies the package:

- From the application's pipeline, after signing in to Azure:

    ```bash
    az functionapp deployment source config-zip \
      --resource-group "$(terraform output -raw resource_group_name)" \
      --name "$(terraform output -raw function_app_name)" \
      --src app.zip
    ```

- Through Terraform: set `function_package_path` to the path of the zip file.
  The package is deployed during `terraform apply` whenever the path changes.

The new package replaces the running version at once.
{%- else %}

### Releasing a new version

Terraform creates the production app, the slot `staging`, one API Management
backend for each of them and two named values in API Management:
`staging-traffic-percent` (starts at `0`) and `function-staging-key`.
The API policy sends that percentage of requests to the staging slot and the
rest to production. Terraform does not change either value after creating
it. A release is done by the application's pipeline:

```bash
RG="$(terraform output -raw resource_group_name)"
APP="$(terraform output -raw function_app_name)"
APIM="$(terraform output -raw api_management_name)"

# 1. Deploy the new package to the staging slot.
az functionapp deployment source config-zip \
  --resource-group "$RG" --name "$APP" --slot staging --src app.zip

# 2. Give the gateway the staging slot's host key. Needed on the first
#    release and after a key of the slot was renewed.
KEY="$(az functionapp keys list --resource-group "$RG" --name "$APP" \
  --slot staging --query functionKeys.default --output tsv)"
az apim nv update --resource-group "$RG" --service-name "$APIM" \
  --named-value-id function-staging-key --secret true --value "$KEY"

# 3. Shift traffic to the staging slot and watch the error rate.
{%- if values.deploymentStrategy == 'canary' %}
az apim nv update --resource-group "$RG" --service-name "$APIM" \
  --named-value-id staging-traffic-percent --value 10
{%- else %}
for percent in 10 25 50 75; do
  az apim nv update --resource-group "$RG" --service-name "$APIM" \
    --named-value-id staging-traffic-percent --value "$percent"
  sleep 600
done
{%- endif %}

# 4. Swap the slots, then send all traffic to production again.
az functionapp deployment slot swap --resource-group "$RG" --name "$APP" \
  --slot staging --target-slot production
az apim nv update --resource-group "$RG" --service-name "$APIM" \
  --named-value-id staging-traffic-percent --value 0
```

To roll back before the swap, set `staging-traffic-percent` to `0`. After
the swap the previous version is in the staging slot: swap again.
{%- if values.deploymentStrategy == 'canary' %}
`canary` gives the new version 10% of the traffic before the swap.
{%- else %}
`linear` raises the share in the steps 10, 25, 50 and 75% (output
`staging_traffic_steps`); the pause between the steps is the pipeline's choice.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Deployment

### State backend

`versions.tf` declares an empty `azurerm` backend. Its settings are passed
to `terraform init`:

```bash
terraform init \
  -backend-config="storage_account_name=<storage account>" \
  -backend-config="container_name=tfstate" \
  -backend-config="key=${{ values.destination.repo }}/terraform.tfstate" \
  -backend-config="use_azuread_auth=true"
```

The storage account and the container must exist; this configuration does
not create them.

### Required permissions

The identity that runs Terraform needs:

- `Contributor` on the subscription (the resource group is created by
  Terraform){% if values.enableStorage %};
- `Role Based Access Control Administrator` (or `User Access Administrator`)
  on the subscription, to give the function's identity access to the blob
  container{% endif %};
- `Storage Blob Data Contributor` on the state container.
{%- if values.authType in ['jwt', 'iam'] %}

The app registration for `api_audience` is managed outside this repository
and needs permissions in Microsoft Entra ID, not in the subscription.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCicd %}

### Deploy workflow

`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` signs in with OpenID Connect (`azure/login`),
plans on pull requests and on `main`, and applies on `main` in the GitHub
environment `production`. It reads:

| Name | Kind | Content |
| ---- | ---- | ------- |
| `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` | secret or variable | Client ID of the app registration or managed identity the workflow signs in as |
| `AZURE_TENANT_ID` | secret or variable | Its tenant |
{%- if values.azureSubscriptionId %}
| subscription | fixed | `${{ values.azureSubscriptionId }}`, the subscription entered when the repository was created. To change it, edit `subscription_id` in `variables.tf` and `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` in the workflow. |
{%- else %}
| `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` | secret or variable | Subscription to deploy into; no subscription was entered when the repository was created |
{%- endif %}
| `TF_STATE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT` | variable | Storage account of the Terraform state |
| `TF_STATE_CONTAINER` | variable, optional | State container, default `tfstate` |
{%- if values.authType == 'oauth2' %}
| `OAUTH2_OPENID_CONFIGURATION_URL` | variable | Value of `oauth2_openid_configuration_url` |
| `OAUTH2_AUDIENCE` | variable | Value of `oauth2_audience` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableAlarms %}
| `ALERT_EMAIL` | variable, optional | Value of `alert_email` |
{%- endif %}

Until every name that is not optional is set, the workflow reports which
ones are missing and skips plan and apply; the run still succeeds.

The identity needs a federated credential for each of these subjects:

- `repo:${{ repo }}:ref:refs/heads/main` (plan on `main`)
- `repo:${{ repo }}:pull_request` (plan on pull requests)
- `repo:${{ repo }}:environment:production` (apply)

The state key is `<repository name>/terraform.tfstate`.
{%- else %}

### Applying

No deployment workflow was requested. Sign in (`az login` or the `ARM_*`
environment variables){% if values.authType == 'oauth2' %}, set `oauth2_openid_configuration_url` and `oauth2_audience`{% endif %}{% if not values.azureSubscriptionId %}, set `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID`{% endif %}, then run
`terraform init` with the backend settings above, `terraform plan` and
`terraform apply`.
{%- endif %}
